          "solution": "Using $\\cos^{-1}x=\\frac\\pi2-\\sin^{-1}x$: $-2\\sin^{-1}x-\\frac\\pi2=\\sin^{-1}(2x+1)$.<br>Take sine of both sides: $-\\cos(2\\sin^{-1}x)=2x+1\\Rightarrow-(1-2x^2)=2x+1\\Rightarrow x^2-x-1=0\\Rightarrow x=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}2$.<br>Domain: $-1\\le x\\le1$ and $-1\\le2x+1\\le1\\Rightarrow-1\\le x\\le0$, so only $x=\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}2$ works (and it satisfies the original equation).<br>$(2x-1)^2=(-\\sqrt5)^2=5$.<br><b>Answer: 5</b>"
